{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.82,"content_digest":"d51e4b4914c68ac423a47634106b27c01fbc03bd1791bbeada42f6e8e1fee331","created_at":"2026-08-23T07:02:07+00:00","direction":"principle_to_meta","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"f4ce88985f3d74410d5db3ceb47104c1d152aa74e7821e822bf78a56fe487730","input_tokens":5890,"latency_ms":33192,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"84613198a4c3fc70c5bc4de5e4d7a4aeb384b452555e522f21bb892165469dd0","output_tokens":2627,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:5480a0a57c8d9c100908b2c1","meta_principle_id":"meta:mathematics-logic:fixed-point","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:scientific-discovery:01M0PPSDF7VG785S8PVM11JJJR","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The existence of a minimax-regret equilibrium in a finite game with mixed strategies is naturally established as a self-consistent strategy profile, i.e. a fixed point of a best-response or regret-minimization correspondence over compact convex mixed-strategy spaces. The meta-principle supplies the general fixed-point framework under which such finite-game equilibrium existence theorems are proved.","relation_type":"depends_on","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T08:08:08+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0PTK9G6V6VXNVFWAWBHMSYT"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T07:02:07+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.72,"content_digest":"da53c868c2726f4c44c9c12bde0b0cd68aa5cd2b5b1ea7d9b65c31d66ddb3b46","created_at":"2026-08-22T20:20:06+00:00","direction":"principle_to_meta","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"c6ee38de06367bd88bfa5d6f64c6546521f7415e8c055db8e339cb84ba6488b2","input_tokens":4695,"latency_ms":17219,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"b933c965511f9ab6c0140a82e59d969747afc0e90ae902802efe591436a86bfb","output_tokens":2313,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:f7ec4ad75caa0f467c7fc7f0","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:credit-assignment","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0NJ2B4B92JEZERKE2YHK525","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The claim describes a concrete architectural mechanism—direct cross-attention from multiple vision encoder layers to the LLM—that improves gradient flow and feature propagation during pre-training. This is a specialized application of the general credit-assignment principle, which explains how layered systems must propagate outcome information back to responsible internal decisions via gradients. The conditions, variables, and scale are compatible: both concern layered neural systems and gradient-based learning, and the claim's convergence outcome is a direct consequence of improved credit assignment.","relation_type":"specializes","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T02:58:57+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0P5WC937PV7PKQMASBZ4D3Z"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T